2026-09-24 — provision the unchanged udmabuf HugeTLB allocation.

RED: two declared policy/command-line assertions fail, zero compile errors.
Frozen udmabuf.c sets MEMFD_SIZE=1024 and page_size=4096*512 for cases 6/7:
the memfd/mmap request is 2 GiB. The ordinary full-suite environment has only
2 GiB guest RAM and reserves one 2 MiB page. Both fresh original replays pass
cases 1..5, fail mmap before case 6, and retain program FAIL.

The small C probe keeps the full 2 GiB request and touches/reads all 1024 pages.
On BOTH retained kernels, with the same 4 GiB HVF machine:
  one reserved hugepage   -> mmap fails ENOMEM (expected RED, exit 1)
  1024 reserved pages     -> mmap plus all page accesses PASS, exit 0
Thus increasing ordinary RAM alone is not sufficient. All four probe guests
shut down normally. No filesystem allocation backs these RAM-only memfds.

Repair: the existing per-program environment selects a separate explicit
4 GiB contract and 1024 reserved 2 MiB pages for drivers/dma-buf:udmabuf only.
Both Images use identical policy. Init verifies the actual reservation;
shell/init parsing remains bounded (1..1024), single-program-only and rejects
malformed/overflowing values. Other programs, the locked 2 GiB K6 machine,
kernel configs, source/binary, assertions and 45-second timeout are unchanged.

Fresh unchanged original on BOTH kernels: 7 PASS / 0 FAIL / 0 SKIP; both
nonce-bound boot/shutdown predicates PASS. Source/binary hashes and snapshot
disks are unchanged. This closes K11c's resource-setup failure, not a compiler
repair, full 970-program campaign or final composition. Old full counts remain.

241 K1 tests PASS, including extracted C init-parser controls at default/64/
512/1024 pages and invalid inputs; Ruff, bash syntax and diff checks PASS.
Changed gate against 991dcbc selects no Rust crate, correctly. Probe and init
build with sealed GCC-14 -static -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror, zero diagnostics.

